          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Mathematics of Maybe: Introduction to Probability"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.845,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "161"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.133,
            0.528,
            0.371
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Did you know? The game of Snakes and Ladders originates in ancient India. It evolved from a traditional dice game called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Jñān-Chaupad̤",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , which was used as an educational tool. Jñān-Chaupad̤ aimed to convey moral lessons, with each ladder symbolising a virtue and each snake representing a vice. Through gameplay, it illustrated the consequences of good and bad behaviour. Various versions of Jñān-Chaupad̤ have been found in different parts of India."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.111,
            0.861,
            0.382
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.596,
                0.111,
                0.861,
                0.382
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.562,
                0.388,
                0.869,
                0.437
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.4: A Jain Jñān-Chaupad̤ game on cloth, National Museum (India, 19th century)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.479,
            0.477,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7.2.2 Theoretical Probability"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.508,
            0.886,
            0.606
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Theoretical probability studies the likelihood of an event happening based on all possible outcomes being "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "equally likely",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". It is what we expect to happen in an ideal, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "perfectly fair situation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In this scenario, no experiment or data is required. Theoretical probability is usually denoted as P (Event) or P (Outcome)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.606,
            0.811,
            0.646
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_6_equation_7.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{Theoretical probability}(\\mathrm{P}) = \\frac{\\mathrm{number of favorable outcomes}}{\\mathrm{ Number of possible outcomes}}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.647,
            0.884,
            0.688
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If you roll a standard 6-sided die, what is the theoretical probability of getting a 4?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.692,
            0.262,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Solution:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.717,
            0.765,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Number of favourable outcomes = 1 (only the number 4)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.741,
            0.835,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Number of all possible outcomes = 6 (the numbers 1 through 6)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.768,
            0.643,
            0.808
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_6_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{rolling} \\; \\mathrm{a} \\; 4)=\\frac{1}{6}=0.1666 \\ldots \\approx 0.167 \\; \\mathrm{or} \\; 16.7 \\%."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.814,
            0.885,
            0.854
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 4:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A letter is picked at random from the word ‘PROBABILITY’. What is the probability of picking the letter B?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
